Mumon wrote:
> All right I've not looked in the code much, but there is alot of it to
> look through. Does "rewarding" actualy do anything? Last I knew there
> was a tameness value. When you feed them (either with throwing it to
> them or droping it) then tameness goes up. When they drop something
> near you tameness goes down. The higher tameness is the more likely
> they are to drop something near you.

Tameness goes up when you feed your pet, but it has nothing to do with
the probability of your pet dopping things near you - only with your
pets chance of going feral.

> Those are what I understand is how pets work. So as near as I can
> figure out, when you take your pet into a shop to steal all the nice
> things, they pick something up and bring it to you, and drop it,
> tameness goes down. You then "reward" your pet, and tameness goes back
> up. Repeat until you've stolen everything.

Nope. Apport goes down when your pet drops something - its tameness
stays the same.

> That's how I understand it, but perhaps I'm wrong, perhaps there is
> something I missed that if you reward a pet right after it drops
> something near you tameness goes up higher than before.#

The tameness doesn't go up higher than usual when you reward your pet
right after it drops something - its apport value does.

> Or perhaps
> there is a trained varible that I don't know about.

Yep, that would be the apport value. See also:
http://www.statslab.cam.ac.uk/~eva/nethack/pets2.html

